  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\n\nSection 13.2.6.4.7 (&#8216;The \"[in body](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#parsing-main-inbody)\" insertion mode&#8217;) says:\n\n>    <dt>An end tag whose tag name is \"sarcasm\"</dt>\n>    <dd>\n>     <p>Take a deep breath, then act as described in the \"any other end\n>     tag\" entry below.</p>\n>    </dd>\n\nIs that serious? <https://github.com/whatwg/fetch/issues/147#issuecomment-152048254> pointed me to this; at least in that context, the answer is clearly &#8216;no&#8217;.\n\nShortly after that, this specification continues:\n\n>    <dt>An end tag whose tag name is one of: \"a\",\n>    \"b\", \"big\", \"code\", \"em\", \"font\", \"i\", \"nobr\", \"s\", \"small\",\n>    \"strike\", \"strong\", \"tt\", \"u\"</dt>\n>    <dd>\n>     <p>Run the <a href='https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#adoption-agency-algorithm'>adoption agency algorithm</a> for the token.</p>\n>    </dd>\n\nIs that serious? Initially, having just seen that `</sarcasm>` joke above, and being unfamiliar with the [adoption agency algorithm](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#adoption-agency-algorithm), I believed that the answer was also &#8216;no&#8217;. But after further reading of the specification, it became clear that the correct answer was &#8216;yes&#8217;.\n\nThe `</sarcasm>` joke is an unnecessary and confusing addition to an already long and complex specification.\n\nWhile trying to check if this issue had already been reported, I found #5382. In that issue, it looks like the original suggestion is serious, but the first reply is a joke. That reaffirms that the `</sarcasm>` joke is creating unnecessary confusion.\n\nThe `</sarcasm>` joke and any similarly confusing jokes should be removed. Humor can still be used effectively in examples, like the one that applies text transformations based on [XKCD1288].",
